    "evidence_text": "15 years to achieve \u201cnear-human performance\u201d on MNIST , ,  and approximately 7 years to surpass humans on ImageNet , , , the GLUE benchmark did not last as long as we would have hoped after the advent of BERT , and rapidly had to be extended into SuperGLUE .\n\nThe speed with which benchmarks become obsolete raises another important question: are current NLU models genuinely as good as their high performance on benchmarks suggests?\n\nA growing body of evidence shows that state-of-the-art models learn to exploit spurious statistical patterns in datasets , , , , , , instead of learning meaning in the flexible and generalizable way that humans do.\n\nWe propose an iterative, adversarial human-and-model-in-the-loop solution for NLU dataset collection that addresses both benchmark longevity and robustness issues.\n\nstate-of-the-art performance on a variety of popular NLI benchmarks.\n\n3) We provide a detailed analysis of the collected data that sheds light on the shortcomings of current models, categorizes the data by inference type to examine weaknesses, and demonstrates good performance on NLI stress tests.\n\nDataset collection\tThe primary aim of this work is to create a new large-scale NLI benchmark on which current state-of-the-art models fail.\n\nWe propose Human-And-Model-in-the-Loop Entailment Training (HAMLET), a training procedure to automatically mitigate problems with current dataset collection procedures (see Figure FIGREF1).\n\nRather\n\nDataset collection ::: Round 1\tFor the first round, we used a BERT-Large model  trained on a concatenation of SNLI  and MNLI , and selected the best-performing model we could train as the starting point for our dataset collection procedure.\n\nFurthermore, our dataset poses new challenges to the community that were less relevant for previous work, such as: can we improve performance online without having to train a new model from scratch every round, how can we overcome catastrophic forgetting, how do we deal with mixed model biases, etc. Because the training set includes examples that the model got right but were not verified, it might be noisy, posing filtering as an additional interesting problem.\n\nFor each round, we report the model error rate, both on verified and unverified examples.\n\nThe\n\nWe obtain state of the art performance on both SNLI and MNLI with the RoBERTa model finetuned on our new data The RoBERTa paper  reports a score of $90.2$ for both MNLI-matched and -mismatched dev, while we obtain $91.0$\n\nIt also implies that BERT, XLNet, and RoBERTa all have different weaknesses, possibly as a function of their training data (BERT, XLNet and RoBERTa were trained on very different data sets, which might or might not have contained information relevant to the weaknesses)\u2014an additional round with a wider model variety would thus be interesting to investigate as a next step.\n\nTo rule out the latter, we trained only on ANLI ($\\sim $163k training examples): doing so with RoBERTa matches the performance of BERT on MNLI when it is trained on the much larger, fully in-domain SNLI+MNLI combined dataset (943k training examples), with both getting $\\sim $86, which is impressive.\n\nHence, this shows that our new challenge test sets are so difficult that the current state-of-the-art model cannot do better than a hypothesis-only prior.\n\nFirst we evaluate two popular existing test sets that were created to expose model weaknesses, and show that our dataset discourages models from learning spurious statistical facts, relative to other large popular datasets\n\nAnalysis ::: Performance on challenge datasets\tRecently, several hard test sets have been made available for revealing the biases NLI models learn from their training datasets , , , .\n\nWe examined model performance on two of these: the SNLI-Hard  test set, which consists of examples that hypothesis-only models label incorrectly, and the NLI stress tests , in which sentences containing antonyms pairs, negations, high word overlap, i.a., are heuristically constructed.\n\nFor comparison, we also report accuracies from the original papers: for SNLI-Hard we present the results from 's implementation of the hierarchical tensor-based Densely Interactive Inference Network  on MNLI, and for the NLI stress tests, we present the performance of 's implementation of InferSent  trained on SNLI.\n\nThis finding is compatible with the idea that the models trained on adversarial data are more impressive and perform better, encouraging writers to devise more creative examples containing harder types of inference in order to stump them.\n\nIn the first visual question answering dataset , biases like \u201c2\u201d being the correct answer to 39% of the questions starting with \u201chow many\u201d allowed learning algorithms to perform well while ignoring the visual modality altogether , .\n\nIn NLI, Gururangan2018annotation, Poliak2018hypothesis and Tsuchiya2018performance showed that hypothesis-only baselines often perform far better than chance.",
